	<h2>What else you get</h2>
	<p>Three things that are not answers to a wall, and one licensing note.</p>

	<h3>Matching cost does not grow with subscriptions</h3>
	<p>
		All active subscriptions live in an in-memory Trie. Finding the subscribers for a topic is a walk down that topic's
		own levels, so the lookup cost tracks how deep the topic is, not how many subscriptions the broker is holding.
		Growing the subscription table does not slow the matching down.
	</p>

	<h3>Shared subscriptions that add throughput</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto implements MQTT 5.0 shared subscriptions and so does TBMQ, so this is not a support difference — it is a
		difference in what adding a member does. Register an
		<a href="/docs/application-shared-subscription/">application shared subscription</a> and the group gets its own Kafka
		topic; each subscribing APPLICATION client joins that topic's consumer group, so an extra instance picks up a share of
		the partitions and the group rebalances when one dies. Messages keep accumulating in Kafka while every member is offline,
		and delivery resumes from the committed offset.
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/shared-subscriptions/">Shared subscriptions</a> covers the DEVICE-client behavior and the load-balancing
		strategies.
	</p>

	<h3>You can change it while it runs, not just watch it</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto 2.1 added a dashboard over the <code>$SYS</code> counters, so seeing broker state is no longer the gap it was.
		What it reports is totals and rates — how many clients are connected, how much traffic moved, how many messages are retained.
		What it cannot tell you is <em>which</em>: there is no client list, no subscription list, no topic browser. And it
		changes nothing, because it is read-only.
	</p>
	<p>
		Configuration is a file, and a reload signal does re-read much of it — but it cannot add or remove a listener, and
		it cannot load or reconfigure a plugin. In 2.1 that second limit reaches further than it sounds, because
		authentication, authorization and persistence are all plugins now. Adding password authentication to a running
		broker is a restart, not a reload: ask for it with a reload and <code>allow_anonymous false</code> takes effect while
		the plugin holding the passwords does not, which locks out everyone. Runtime change is limited to authentication and authorization,
		driven over <code>$CONTROL</code> topics with <code>mosquitto_ctrl</code>.
	</p>
	<p>
		In TBMQ those are entities, not file entries. <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/mqtt-client-credentials/">Credentials</a> and
		topic rules, <a href="/docs/integrations/">integrations</a>,
		<a href="/docs/application-shared-subscription/">shared subscriptions</a>, blocked clients and
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/users/">users</a> are created and edited while the cluster runs, from the
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/monitoring/">web UI</a> or the <a href="/docs/rest-api/">REST API</a>, with no restart
		and no file to ship to every node. The same views answer the operational questions — which
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions</a> are connected and what each is subscribed to, which connection attempts
		were rejected and why — plus a built-in
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/websocket-client/">WebSocket client</a> for publishing a test message without leaving the
		browser, and metrics export to <a href="/docs/prometheus-metrics/">Prometheus</a>. Broker-level tuning is a config
		file and a restart in both, and TBMQ is no different there.
	</p>

	<h3>Open source, and what Professional Edition adds</h3>
	<p>
		Everything above is Community Edition, <a href="https://github.com/thingsboard/tbmq">Apache 2.0</a>, commercial use
		included. <a href="/pricing/">Professional Edition</a> adds OAuth 2.0 single sign-on, role-based access control, audit
		logs, domain management and white labeling — on the same broker, not a different one.
	</p>

	<h2>What the architecture is built to do</h2>
	<p>Each figure comes from a published test, with its hardware and load profile documented.</p>

	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-split-2" title="3M msg/sec delivered">
			fan-out from a single 32-vCPU node — 1k msg/sec in, 3M out (100 publishers → 3,000 subscribers, QoS 0), 7.4 ms
			average latency, 54% CPU.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:plug-connected" title="100M connections">
			held on a 25-node cluster across 100M unique topics, while moving 6M messages per second at QoS 1.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-right-left" title="1M msg/sec point-to-point">
			500,000 publishers to 500,000 persistent device subscribers at QoS 1, on five nodes.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

	<p>
		On methodology: these are TBMQ tests on TBMQ hardware, not a same-hardware comparison.
		<strong>Mosquitto publishes no benchmark results</strong> — the 2.0 announcement said some were planned and none followed
		— so there is no authoritative head-to-head number to quote, from us or anyone else.
	</p>
	<p><a href="/performance/">Full benchmark methodology and results →</a></p>

	<h2>At a glance</h2>
	<div class="overflow-x">
		<table>
			<thead>
