		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Every Mosquitto column above describes the open-source Eclipse broker at its defaults. Several of these gaps are
		things Cedalo's commercial Pro Edition fills; none of them are things open-source Mosquitto claims to do.
	</p>

	<h2 id="stay">Where Mosquitto still fits</h2>
	<p>
		Three cases where the move is not worth making yet. If your deployment is a few thousand clients on one host with no
		availability requirement beyond restarting a service, TBMQ's Kafka, Redis and PostgreSQL dependencies cost you
		operational surface and buy you nothing. If the hardware is a gateway or an edge box with a few hundred megabytes of
		RAM, TBMQ is not built for that envelope — though a small broker there can still bridge into a central TBMQ cluster,
		which is the usual shape once there is more than one site. And if you want the protocol and nothing else — no
		cluster, no admin API, no integrations — the extra moving parts are a cost rather than a feature.
	</p>

	<h2>Moving over without a flag day</h2>
	<p>
		You do not have to cut over at once, and the two directions come from different sides. TBMQ's MQTT Integration is
		<em>outbound</em> — it bridges TBMQ to an external broker — so the inbound leg uses Mosquitto's own bridge.
	</p>

	<MqttDiagram caption="Both legs run at once, in opposite directions, for as long as the migration takes.">
